---
/**
 * Who runs this site, what it claims, and what stands in place of a credential.
 *
 * The argument is deliberate. A page about retirement money with no named author is
 * worth nothing, and a page that invents a credential, a review board or a "reviewed
 * by" line is worth less than nothing, because a reader who catches one invention has
 * to throw out everything else too. What this project has instead is a public
 * repository, specifications frozen before their results were seen, a ledger that
 * records the failures, and a corrections log. Weaker than a licence, and checkable.
 *
 * The AI disclosure is deliberately short here and long on `/methodology/`. This page
 * says it happened; that page says what it means for the numbers. Keep it that way, and
 * do not let the two grow into duplicates of each other.
 *
 * Counts come from `uv run python -m portfolio_edge.reporting.programme_status`, which
 * reads the ledger. Re-read it before editing a number here.
 */
import Callout from "~/components/Callout.astro";
import Figure from "~/components/Figure.astro";
import MarginNote from "~/components/MarginNote.astro";
import Base from "~/layouts/Base.astro";
import { REPO_URL } from "~/lib/site";
---

<Base
  title="About this site and the person who writes it"
  description="Sean Flanagan writes this. He holds no financial licence and no professional credential, the site sells nothing, and what stands in place of a credential is a public repository, frozen specifications, a ledger of failed attempts and a corrections log."
>
  <div class="prose">
    <h1 class="font-serif text-4xl tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink">About this site and the person who writes it</h1>

    <MarginNote label="The question">
      <p>
        Who wrote this, and why believe any of it? Checked on <span data-numeric>2026-08-23</span>.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>One person writes this. Sean Flanagan, a software engineer in the United States, working in his own time.
    He holds no financial licence and no credential of any kind, and you should know that before the first
    number.</p>

    <p>The site is the reading half of a public repository. The measuring half is Python:
    <span data-numeric>23</span> experiment families, <span data-numeric>27</span> frozen specifications and a
    ledger with <span data-numeric>134</span> entries. Every number links back to the file that owns it. If that
    file does not say what the sentence says, the sentence is wrong, and an
    <a href={`${REPO_URL}/issues`} rel="noopener noreferrer">issue on GitHub</a> is the fastest way to say so.</p>

  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">

    <h2>Who writes this</h2>

    <MarginNote label="No badge">
      <p>
        No review board, no editorial panel, and no "reviewed by" line. A site that claims a credential it does
        not hold has told you what its other claims are worth.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>He is not a registered investment adviser. He does not work for a broker-dealer or an advisory firm, he is
    not a financial planner, and he holds no CFA, CFP, CPA, Series 7 or Series 65. No licence, no firm, no
    professional body, and nobody reviews a page before it goes up. No employer and no client is anywhere near
    this work.</p>

    <p>AI was used, heavily. The code, the research and these sentences were drafted by a large language model,
    then cut by a person who is responsible for what they say. <a href="/methodology/">How a result earns its
    status</a> says what gets checked by machinery rather than by another model, and what none of it protects you
    from.</p>

  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">

    <h2>What stands in place of a credential</h2>

    <p>Shown work, and a great deal of it.</p>

    <ul>
      <li>The <a href={REPO_URL} rel="noopener noreferrer">repository is public</a>. That includes the commits
      that undo the previous week.</li>
      <li>Each of the <span data-numeric>27</span> specifications was frozen before anyone saw the result, which
      is what stops a bar from being moved to meet a number.</li>
      <li>The ledger records every attempt that carried a hypothesis, including the abandoned ones. Nine runs
      ended in a rejection and nine ended in "we could not tell".</li>
      <li>Thirty-three errors have been found in this project's own numbers, and each one is on
      <a href="/corrections/">the corrections log</a> with its date and what caught it.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>None of that makes a conclusion correct. It makes each one checkable, which is the only offer anyone
    without a licence can honestly make.</p>

  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">

    <h2>How it is paid for</h2>

    <p>Nothing pays for it. No advertising. No affiliate links. No sponsors, no paid placement, no compensation
    from any fund company, broker, custodian or data provider. Nothing to subscribe to and nothing to buy. Hosting
    costs a few dollars a month and comes out of the author's own pocket.</p>

    <MarginNote label="Where the interests are listed">
      <p>
        What the author owns is not published. <a href="/disclosures/#holdings">The disclosures page</a> says so,
        and lists the interests that do exist.
      </p>
    </MarginNote>

    <p>A site with nothing to sell can still be wrong. Having no advertiser and no product removes one way of
    being wrong out of many, and the rest are still live.</p>

  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">

    <h2>What the site claims, and what it refuses to</h2>

    <p>The reliable win is fees, taxes and which account each fund sits in. That is worth about
    <Figure id="structural-budget" inline /> against the portfolio an ordinary investor would otherwise hold, it
    needs no forecast, and your own account would show it inside a year.</p>

    <p>Beating a cheap index fund is a different question, and we cannot show that we do. The defensible advantage
    is <Figure id="edge-vs-cheap-index" inline />, against <Figure id="index-tracking-error" inline /> of
    year-to-year wander away from that index. Everything past that is mostly "we can't tell", which is a verdict
    rather than an evasion. <a href="/how-sure/">How sure we are</a> explains the difference.</p>

    <Callout kind="caveat">
      <p>
        Nothing here has been checked by a securities lawyer, a tax adviser or an accountant. The tax arithmetic
        assumes a US federal individual investor and ignores every state, and a US-specific tax result is often
        exactly backwards somewhere else.
      </p>
    </Callout>

  </div>

  <div class="prose mt-14">

    <h2>The one thing this site will never do</h2>

    <p>It will never answer a question about your portfolio. Not by email, not in an issue, not privately. Nobody
    here knows your tax bracket, your job security, what you owe or when you stop working, and a general answer
    dressed as a personal one is worse than no answer, because you would act on it.
    <a href="/disclaimer/">The disclaimer</a> says the rest of it. Questions about the method, the data or an
    error in a number are welcome anywhere.</p>

  </div>

</Base>
